  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1175",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "58c547c90cb5f3095bb72d38db9a1540c2fdb89c603850271cff116b190642bd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-58c547c90cb5-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Wednesday, November 7, 2012 The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:06 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1175",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "58c547c90cb5f3095bb72d38db9a1540c2fdb89c603850271cff116b190642bd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-58c547c90cb5-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. ZIEVE: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: Rule 54(d) provides a standard for an award of costs to a prevailing party that, by the Rule's express terms, does not apply where Federal statute provides otherwise. The Fair Debt Collection Practices Act provides otherwise because it states a different rule for awarding costs than does Rule 54(d)."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1175",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "58c547c90cb5f3095bb72d38db9a1540c2fdb89c603850271cff116b190642bd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-58c547c90cb5-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Under -- under that provision, that's certainly true. You can't -- you can't get costs under that provision unless there has been that prerequisite. But it's -- you know, it's ancient law that repeals by implication are not favored. And what you're arguing here is that that provision effectively repeals another provision which allows costs in all cases, whether or not there has been misbehavior. Now, why -- why is this an exception to our general rule? I just don't -- this doesn't seem to me like a clear repealer."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1175",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "58c547c90cb5f3095bb72d38db9a1540c2fdb89c603850271cff116b190642bd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-58c547c90cb5-0000-0005",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 9,
        "start": 9
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Congress wanted to ensure that meritorious suits by impecunious debtors were not deterred by the prospect that an award of costs would exceed the value of the damage that could be recovered in a successful suit."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1175",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "58c547c90cb5f3095bb72d38db9a1540c2fdb89c603850271cff116b190642bd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-58c547c90cb5-0000-0004",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 10,
        "start": 10
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The FTC got about 120,000 complaints from consumers about debt collectors last year, more than any other industry."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (amicus curiae)",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1175",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "58c547c90cb5f3095bb72d38db9a1540c2fdb89c603850271cff116b190642bd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-58c547c90cb5-0000-0002",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 13,
        "start": 13
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. FEIGIN: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: Rule 54(d) expressly codifies in absolute form the well-established principle that a specific provision displaces a more general one."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1175",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "58c547c90cb5f3095bb72d38db9a1540c2fdb89c603850271cff116b190642bd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-58c547c90cb5-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Did you look up -- did you try to do any sampling on that? Because I did, actually, and -- and I discovered something that I think is not as strong for you, but it isn't too much against"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1175",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "58c547c90cb5f3095bb72d38db9a1540c2fdb89c603850271cff116b190642bd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-58c547c90cb5-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Am I to understand your simple position to be that what Rule 54(d) does -- says is if another provision deals with costs, you're relegated to that other provision?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Lisa S. Blatt",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1175",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "58c547c90cb5f3095bb72d38db9a1540c2fdb89c603850271cff116b190642bd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-58c547c90cb5-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Our position is that the second sentence of section 1692k(a)(3) is a pro-defendant provision that does not strip courts of their discretion under Rule 54 to award costs to prevailing defendants."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1175",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "58c547c90cb5f3095bb72d38db9a1540c2fdb89c603850271cff116b190642bd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-58c547c90cb5-0001-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "So this provision says, on a finding by the court that it's brought in bad faith, the court may award to the defendant attorney's fees and costs. So suppose a defendant wins, but there's not a finding that it was made in bad faith, would then the person be entitled to either attorney's fees or costs?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1175",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "58c547c90cb5f3095bb72d38db9a1540c2fdb89c603850271cff116b190642bd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-58c547c90cb5-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: Okay. So if you would not get costs under that provision --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Blatt",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1175",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "58c547c90cb5f3095bb72d38db9a1540c2fdb89c603850271cff116b190642bd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-58c547c90cb5-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. BLATT: Because -- because, again, Rule 54 is not preemption -- a field preemption. It's saying if Congress intended to displace -- the proviso, unless otherwise provided, it was recognition that other statutes might displace Rule 54. And if you look at all the statutes that we cite on pages 19 and 20, they actually do prohibit costs."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1175",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "58c547c90cb5f3095bb72d38db9a1540c2fdb89c603850271cff116b190642bd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-58c547c90cb5-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Counsel, it was thinking about prevailing parties because the predecessor sentence --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1175",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "58c547c90cb5f3095bb72d38db9a1540c2fdb89c603850271cff116b190642bd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-58c547c90cb5-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: I guess, in the first sentence of 3, the phrase \"the costs of the action\" is really superfluous in light of 54(d)(1). You really don't know that."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1175",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "58c547c90cb5f3095bb72d38db9a1540c2fdb89c603850271cff116b190642bd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-58c547c90cb5-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Thank you, counsel. Ms. Zieve, you have six minutes remaining."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Zieve",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1175",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "58c547c90cb5f3095bb72d38db9a1540c2fdb89c603850271cff116b190642bd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-58c547c90cb5-0002-0006",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "behind that hypothetical is that the one child is going to get their allowance no matter what. The presumption here is that Rule 54(d) will apply unless a statute provides otherwise. This statute doesn't."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1175",
